1
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:07,812
<i>The world is full of coincidences.</i>

2
00:00:09,214 --> 00:00:10,381
<i>Twenty-five years ago,</i>

3
00:00:11,316 --> 00:00:13,718
<i>I had a fateful encounter with Mariko.</i>

4
00:00:14,552 --> 00:00:17,355
<i>I've always just watched over here.</i>

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:20,625
<i>But now, she is finally aware of me.</i>

6
00:00:21,793 --> 00:00:23,561
<i>Coincidences can be manufactured.</i>

7
00:00:24,429 --> 00:00:27,065
<i>A coincidence meant to make Mariko smile.</i>

8
00:00:28,266 --> 00:00:29,200
<i>However,</i>

9
00:00:30,602 --> 00:00:35,406
<i>there are plenty of people who approach</i>
<i>Mariko and those who envy her.</i>

10
00:00:36,107 --> 00:00:38,743
They say Mr. Tateiwa was killed.

11
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:41,513
<i>I must do what I need to.</i>

12
00:00:41,579 --> 00:00:42,614
Who is this man…

13
00:00:44,149 --> 00:00:46,284
<i>Because I'm the one by Mariko's side…</i>

14
00:00:48,553 --> 00:00:49,521
<i>protecting her.</i>

15
00:00:51,656 --> 00:00:52,724
Excuse me.

16
00:00:54,559 --> 00:00:57,295
May I speak with you for a moment?

17
00:00:57,362 --> 00:00:58,496
INSPECTOR TAISUKE SAKURA

18
00:01:00,965 --> 00:01:01,799
Yes.

19
00:01:01,866 --> 00:01:05,703
Where were you
on July 7, around 10:25 p.m.?

20
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:09,941
Is that when the thing
with Mr. Tateiwa happened?

21
00:01:11,342 --> 00:01:12,177
Yes.

22
00:01:12,243 --> 00:01:14,579
Was I caught on a security camera?

23
00:01:17,382 --> 00:01:20,385
That's probably what
the police have gathered.

24
00:01:21,319 --> 00:01:22,320
So, what?

25
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:25,957
I was meeting with Tateiwa at the place
on the news where the incident happened.

26
00:01:34,999 --> 00:01:36,868
What were you doing there?

27
00:01:39,337 --> 00:01:41,239
We were talking about work.

28
00:01:41,306 --> 00:01:44,275
In a place like that,
outside of working hours?

29
00:01:44,342 --> 00:01:46,544
Yes, that is correct.

30
00:01:47,946 --> 00:01:52,584
Immediately after meeting with you,
Tateiwa was murdered by someone.

31
00:01:55,220 --> 00:01:56,221
I am shocked.

32
00:01:59,157 --> 00:02:03,695
Normally, you wouldn't have
any contact with the victim, Mr. Tateiwa,

33
00:02:04,395 --> 00:02:06,497
who is a director, right?

34
00:02:06,998 --> 00:02:09,567
-What specifically were you talking about?
-Uh…

35
00:02:10,735 --> 00:02:12,637
It wasn't on camera, was it?

36
00:02:14,339 --> 00:02:16,140
The part where I killed Tateiwa.

37
00:02:17,642 --> 00:02:22,046
Since it wasn't on camera,
that means I didn't do it.

38
00:02:43,701 --> 00:02:44,902
TOKYO POLICE TAISUKE SAKURA

39
00:02:44,969 --> 00:02:46,204
INSPECTOR

40
00:02:47,772 --> 00:02:49,807
THANKS FOR WATCHING THE DRAMA
"BACK TO SQUARE ONE, DETECTIVE"

41
00:02:53,044 --> 00:02:54,746
PUBLIC ORDER MAINTENANCE

42
00:02:56,781 --> 00:02:58,716
TAISUKE SAKURA

43
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:00,385
AWARD

44
00:03:01,486 --> 00:03:03,087
MERITORIOUS SERVICE AWARD

45
00:03:03,154 --> 00:03:05,657
TAISUKE SAKURA

46
00:03:07,759 --> 00:03:09,527
<i>There are things I must do.</i>

47
00:03:20,405 --> 00:03:21,706
-I'm first!
-I'm second.

48
00:03:22,907 --> 00:03:23,775
It's cold!

49
00:03:27,912 --> 00:03:29,113
Can you reach out your hand?

50
00:03:31,749 --> 00:03:32,884
It's okay.

51
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:34,819
It's not scary if we hold hands.

52
00:03:37,855 --> 00:03:38,690
Let's go.

53
00:03:39,324 --> 00:03:40,658
Ready, and…

54
00:03:49,534 --> 00:03:51,469
The cold water feels great.

55
00:04:05,249 --> 00:04:06,918
I've seen it before.

56
00:04:08,019 --> 00:04:09,721
I've seen Mariko's smile before.

57
00:04:11,255 --> 00:04:12,323
Oh.

58
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:14,125
But that was different.

59
00:04:15,526 --> 00:04:16,394
What was?

60
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:19,731
The power of it.

61
00:04:20,765 --> 00:04:21,599
Thank you.

62
00:04:23,768 --> 00:04:24,635
<i>She…</i>

63
00:04:26,437 --> 00:04:28,206
smiled at me…

64
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:31,609
At me…

65
00:04:35,446 --> 00:04:37,882
So you're saying that peeking at a smile
meant for someone else

66
00:04:37,949 --> 00:04:44,055
is different in impact compared
to a smile directed at you?

67
00:04:44,122 --> 00:04:45,056
It was so cute.

68
00:04:47,558 --> 00:04:49,026
Oh, no, not like that.

69
00:04:49,093 --> 00:04:51,429
I'm not talking
about her looks or anything.

70
00:04:51,496 --> 00:04:52,930
I get it, I get it.

71
00:04:53,898 --> 00:04:54,766
I understand.

72
00:04:55,533 --> 00:04:57,368
You want to be the only one

73
00:04:57,435 --> 00:05:00,471
who can make her smile
and the only one who gets to see it.

74
00:05:01,506 --> 00:05:04,342
And you'll start wanting more things
that are just for you.

75
00:05:05,143 --> 00:05:07,245
People call that possessiveness.

76
00:05:08,946 --> 00:05:10,615
Well, it's a natural part of love.

77
00:05:11,783 --> 00:05:13,317
-What, for you too?
-No, not me.

78
00:05:14,685 --> 00:05:16,687
I've never been that obsessed
with a single person.

79
00:05:18,523 --> 00:05:20,792
But it's like that for you, right?

80
00:05:21,826 --> 00:05:28,733
I'm sure that soon, you'll start hating it

81
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:30,735
when she smiles at other people.

82
00:05:34,372 --> 00:05:36,307
Be careful.

83
00:05:38,309 --> 00:05:39,911
<i>No way that will happen.</i>

84
00:05:50,354 --> 00:05:53,090
<i>I want Mariko's life to be happy.</i>

85
00:05:54,258 --> 00:05:55,259
<i>So,</i>

86
00:05:55,326 --> 00:05:59,931
<i>I want her to always be smiling,</i>
<i>no matter who she's with.</i>

87
00:06:01,699 --> 00:06:02,600
<i>But…</i>

88
00:06:03,367 --> 00:06:05,336
NOSE COSMETICS

89
00:06:07,371 --> 00:06:10,775
<i>Lately, Mariko is far from smiling.</i>

90
00:06:10,842 --> 00:06:12,810
<i>She's in an extremely bad mood.</i>

91
00:06:14,545 --> 00:06:15,913
<i>Why?</i>

92
00:06:18,182 --> 00:06:21,052
<i>Because her birthday is approaching.</i>

93
00:06:23,087 --> 00:06:26,624
<i>More precisely, the annual</i>
<i>family birthday celebration</i>

94
00:06:26,691 --> 00:06:28,493
<i>at her parents' house is approaching.</i>

95
00:06:31,496 --> 00:06:32,396
Here you go.

96
00:06:33,197 --> 00:06:34,232
Thank you.

97
00:06:34,999 --> 00:06:37,368
-You really don't have to do this.
-No, I was making one anyway.

98
00:06:57,421 --> 00:06:58,956
-Izumi.
-Yes?

99
00:06:59,023 --> 00:07:01,392
Will you come to my family's house
with me the day after tomorrow?

100
00:07:01,459 --> 00:07:03,961
There's no way I can go.

101
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:07,031
I…

102
00:07:08,599 --> 00:07:10,268
don't like her.

103
00:07:11,035 --> 00:07:12,270
The president's wife, I mean.

104
00:07:13,738 --> 00:07:16,107
Not that I dislike the president, though.

105
00:07:16,173 --> 00:07:18,543
He's a good person. Kind too.

106
00:07:20,044 --> 00:07:22,146
Who knows? I don't know him well.

107
00:07:22,213 --> 00:07:24,549
There you go again.

108
00:07:25,550 --> 00:07:28,886
Maybe it's just that you don't
open up enough, Mariko?

109
00:07:30,288 --> 00:07:34,025
If you showed your true feelings more,
wouldn't you get closer?

110
00:07:34,091 --> 00:07:36,093
If you could open up to someone,

111
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,796
it would ease your mind more too, Mariko.

112
00:07:39,830 --> 00:07:40,998
Really?

113
00:07:41,065 --> 00:07:42,166
Yes.

114
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:45,236
Well, I'm actually not good
at sharing things with people.

115
00:07:45,803 --> 00:07:47,838
What? Really?

116
00:07:50,908 --> 00:07:51,976
Any progress on the project?

117
00:07:52,043 --> 00:07:53,110
Speak of the devil…

118
00:07:53,177 --> 00:07:55,079
-I will check on it.
-Thank you.

119
00:07:58,316 --> 00:07:59,817
<i>Outside of work,</i>

120
00:07:59,884 --> 00:08:03,287
<i>Mariko and the president</i>
<i>have almost no communication.</i>

121
00:08:04,822 --> 00:08:09,694
<i>I've watched many types</i>
<i>of parents and children, but…</i>

122
00:08:11,128 --> 00:08:13,631
I heard Hatano was released
from prison, right?

123
00:08:13,698 --> 00:08:15,099
Be careful.

124
00:08:15,166 --> 00:08:17,935
It was a long time ago, but he might talk
about things involving you too.

125
00:08:18,002 --> 00:08:19,003
GINJIRO NOSE

126
00:08:20,171 --> 00:08:21,906
YEAR 6, CLASS 3
NO. 1 GOLD STAR CLASS

127
00:08:21,973 --> 00:08:23,641
SATORU HATANO

128
00:08:41,058 --> 00:08:41,892
GINJIRO NOSE

129
00:08:41,959 --> 00:08:44,495
<i>Is the issue the father as an individual?</i>

130
00:08:45,529 --> 00:08:48,132
<i>Or is it the whole family</i>
<i>as a collective unit?</i>

131
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,569
<i>I want Mariko to smile.</i>

132
00:08:53,704 --> 00:08:54,705
<i>So,</i>

133
00:08:55,806 --> 00:08:58,342
<i>I must eliminate the risk factors</i>

134
00:08:58,909 --> 00:09:00,011
<i>that stand in the way.</i>

135
00:09:22,033 --> 00:09:23,567
I'm home, Mom.

136
00:09:46,323 --> 00:09:47,558
Mariko?

137
00:09:48,192 --> 00:09:49,794
Hi, I'm back.

138
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:50,728
Welcome home.

139
00:09:50,795 --> 00:09:54,465
Your father is out for a bit right now,
but we'll start as soon as he returns.

140
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:10,748
Yes, hello?

141
00:10:47,218 --> 00:10:49,420
Sorry, I know it's your day off.

142
00:10:49,487 --> 00:10:52,056
I didn't know you were
at your parents' before I called.

143
00:10:53,190 --> 00:10:54,759
I should have waited
until the beginning of the week.

144
00:10:55,793 --> 00:10:58,195
It'll cause trouble
for their side if it's late.

145
00:10:58,863 --> 00:11:00,231
-Wait here a second.
-Okay.

146
00:11:04,602 --> 00:11:06,871
Oh, can I help you?

147
00:11:06,937 --> 00:11:10,641
Chairwoman! I am Yukimura
from the Brand Business Department.

148
00:11:10,708 --> 00:11:14,345
I forgot to have the samples
confirmed yesterday.

149
00:11:14,411 --> 00:11:17,915
Oh, well, thank you. Anyway, come in.

150
00:11:17,982 --> 00:11:19,250
Oh, no, no.

151
00:11:19,316 --> 00:11:21,418
I'll be leaving soon. I'll just wait here.

152
00:11:21,485 --> 00:11:22,653
No, it's okay.

153
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:26,223
In the old days, all the employees
would often come over to eat.

154
00:11:27,091 --> 00:11:29,860
Come on, quickly!

155
00:11:37,067 --> 00:11:40,638
I definitely prefer
<i>The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle</i> too.

156
00:11:40,704 --> 00:11:42,139
Right!

157
00:11:42,206 --> 00:11:44,642
Well, <i>Norwegian Wood</i> is good too,

158
00:11:44,708 --> 00:11:47,411
but this is definitely
his true masterpiece.

159
00:11:48,078 --> 00:11:49,213
<i>Mariko's grandmother</i>

160
00:11:49,780 --> 00:11:51,348
<i>likes Haruki Murakami.</i>

161
00:11:52,049 --> 00:11:53,017
<i>On the release day of a new book…</i>

162
00:11:54,018 --> 00:11:56,854
<i>…she is a die-hard fan</i>
<i>who queues at bookstores</i>

163
00:11:56,921 --> 00:11:59,623
<i>for hours before they open to get a copy.</i>

164
00:12:00,191 --> 00:12:02,993
It is said that in that work,
the expression of evil,

165
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:06,030
which had been fantastical until then,
began to take shape as real evil

166
00:12:06,096 --> 00:12:07,464
accompanied by history and physicality.

167
00:12:07,531 --> 00:12:09,200
Yes, exactly!

168
00:12:10,201 --> 00:12:11,635
Oh, have you read the new one?

169
00:12:11,702 --> 00:12:13,003
No, not yet.

170
00:12:13,737 --> 00:12:14,872
I'll lend it to you then.

171
00:12:15,739 --> 00:12:16,874
-What, really?
-Yes.

172
00:12:16,941 --> 00:12:18,375
Wow, thank you so much.

173
00:12:22,046 --> 00:12:23,080
-Here.
-Yes.

174
00:12:23,848 --> 00:12:24,949
Thank you very much.

175
00:12:27,384 --> 00:12:28,452
Well, I'll be going now.

176
00:12:29,019 --> 00:12:30,888
Already? Stay for lunch.

177
00:12:32,156 --> 00:12:35,292
Mom, that's too short notice for him.

178
00:12:35,359 --> 00:12:37,127
Do you have plans after this?

179
00:12:37,194 --> 00:12:41,665
No, well, I don't, but…

180
00:12:43,133 --> 00:12:44,501
Eat with us, why not?

181
00:12:44,568 --> 00:12:45,669
What?

182
00:12:45,736 --> 00:12:48,973
You can just email this
after work today, right?

183
00:12:49,974 --> 00:12:51,308
But…

184
00:12:54,311 --> 00:12:56,113
Man, I'm starving.

185
00:12:57,281 --> 00:12:58,115
Who are you?

186
00:13:00,150 --> 00:13:02,419
Come, sit here.

187
00:13:02,486 --> 00:13:03,854
Ah, excuse me.

188
00:13:03,921 --> 00:13:05,356
I'm home.

189
00:13:05,890 --> 00:13:07,091
-Welcome back.
-Welcome home.

190
00:13:07,157 --> 00:13:08,025
President…

191
00:13:08,692 --> 00:13:11,462
Thank you for the other day.

192
00:13:11,528 --> 00:13:14,398
Oh, Yukimura. Hello.

193
00:13:15,532 --> 00:13:16,567
Welcome home.

194
00:13:18,102 --> 00:13:19,336
I'll go wash my hands.

195
00:13:31,749 --> 00:13:34,351
Mariko, have this too. You like it, right?

196
00:13:35,152 --> 00:13:36,220
Thank you very much.

197
00:13:38,122 --> 00:13:41,325
<i>Mariko's stepmother, Sayuri,</i>
<i>came into the family</i>

198
00:13:42,092 --> 00:13:43,594
<i>when Mariko was seven years old.</i>

199
00:13:43,661 --> 00:13:46,096
THERE'S A NEW LADY THERE NOW

200
00:13:51,168 --> 00:13:54,004
<i>She entered the family less than a year</i>

201
00:13:54,571 --> 00:13:56,240
<i>after Mariko's mother passed away.</i>

202
00:13:56,307 --> 00:13:57,441
THANK YOU FOR THE 25 YEARS
#MARRIAGEANNIVERSARY

203
00:13:57,508 --> 00:14:00,878
With Mariko being so outstanding,
our company is in safe hands.

204
00:14:00,945 --> 00:14:02,680
It's not your company.

205
00:14:04,782 --> 00:14:08,018
<i>She is clearly</i>
<i>someone who's just after money.</i>

206
00:14:10,187 --> 00:14:15,893
<i>No, it's normal for obvious things</i>
<i>to have especially complex layers to them.</i>

207
00:14:16,927 --> 00:14:18,395
<i>We must not let our guard down.</i>

208
00:14:18,462 --> 00:14:23,634
You're making a show
of cooking Kyoko's specialty dish.

209
00:14:24,868 --> 00:14:27,004
You have a lot of nerve, honestly.

210
00:14:29,006 --> 00:14:31,041
Oh, come now, Mother.

211
00:14:31,108 --> 00:14:35,512
This is a recipe I taught Kyoko.

212
00:14:35,579 --> 00:14:39,950
I made it with Kyoko and Mariko
when she was a kid, right?

213
00:14:40,017 --> 00:14:41,652
<i>This is the first I've heard of it.</i>

214
00:14:41,719 --> 00:14:47,458
<i>This woman and Mariko's mother</i>
<i>were old acquaintances.</i>

215
00:14:47,524 --> 00:14:49,893
Mariko, when Keito joins
the company next year,

216
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:51,996
take care of him, okay?

217
00:14:52,730 --> 00:14:54,765
What? No way.

218
00:14:54,832 --> 00:14:56,500
I'm not working under her.

219
00:14:57,334 --> 00:14:59,236
Isn't my sister scary?

220
00:15:00,104 --> 00:15:02,706
Well, no, she isn't.

221
00:15:02,773 --> 00:15:04,475
More importantly, Keito,

222
00:15:05,042 --> 00:15:07,111
are you sure you're going
to graduate from univeristy?

223
00:15:07,177 --> 00:15:09,013
Well, it'll work out somehow.

224
00:15:09,079 --> 00:15:11,749
-It won't work out if you don't work hard.
-<i>My alarm</i>

225
00:15:11,815 --> 00:15:13,717
-<i>isn't triggered by you for now.</i>
-All right, all right,

226
00:15:13,784 --> 00:15:14,885
I'll do my best.

227
00:15:15,519 --> 00:15:20,657
<i>Keito, I sense that Mariko</i>
<i>actually cares for you in her own way.</i>

228
00:15:23,527 --> 00:15:25,462
<i>So he's the problem after all.</i>

229
00:15:27,131 --> 00:15:28,599
Yukimura.

230
00:15:28,665 --> 00:15:29,666
Yes.

231
00:15:29,733 --> 00:15:32,403
Mariko has strong preferences,

232
00:15:32,469 --> 00:15:35,205
so she isn't causing trouble for everyone
in the Brand Business Department, is she?

233
00:15:35,272 --> 00:15:36,607
Not at all.

234
00:15:36,673 --> 00:15:38,409
Everyone is motivated and working hard.

235
00:15:39,143 --> 00:15:40,511
-Is that so?
-Yes.

236
00:15:41,745 --> 00:15:44,581
So this is the young employee
you played golf with

237
00:15:44,648 --> 00:15:46,150
the other day.

238
00:15:46,950 --> 00:15:48,719
Yukimura is very good at golf.

239
00:15:48,786 --> 00:15:50,254
He's also polite.

240
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:51,488
Let's go again soon.

241
00:15:51,555 --> 00:15:52,990
I would be honored.

242
00:15:55,926 --> 00:15:58,095
When did you join Mariko's department?

243
00:15:58,162 --> 00:15:59,997
I didn't know at all.

244
00:16:00,064 --> 00:16:01,465
It's only been about two weeks.

245
00:16:03,801 --> 00:16:07,805
Since Mariko selected you,
you must be very capable, huh?

246
00:16:10,474 --> 00:16:14,244
Mariko doesn't speak much,

247
00:16:14,311 --> 00:16:17,181
so you sometimes find it difficult
to know what she's thinking, right?

248
00:16:17,247 --> 00:16:19,316
No, it's not a problem.

249
00:16:20,150 --> 00:16:22,319
She has been cool since she was a child,

250
00:16:22,386 --> 00:16:25,355
and she almost never
complains or acts spoiled.

251
00:16:26,056 --> 00:16:29,393
As her father, I often don't know
what she's thinking either.

252
00:16:31,795 --> 00:16:35,099
Give Mariko your support.

253
00:16:35,165 --> 00:16:38,602
Oh, well, it would be my honor.

254
00:16:38,669 --> 00:16:41,038
You don't need to say all of that.

255
00:16:55,219 --> 00:16:57,454
-We're back.
-Hello.

256
00:16:57,521 --> 00:16:59,423
That Sota Yukimura guy you're hounding

257
00:16:59,490 --> 00:17:01,892
doesn't have much
of a presence at his company.

258
00:17:01,959 --> 00:17:03,660
Nobody knows much about him.

259
00:17:03,727 --> 00:17:07,498
All we know is he was transferred
to the Brand Department recently.

260
00:17:08,065 --> 00:17:09,299
-Transferred?
-Well,

261
00:17:09,366 --> 00:17:11,435
the president's daughter
selected him specially.

262
00:17:11,502 --> 00:17:12,803
It's unprecedented.

263
00:17:14,705 --> 00:17:16,373
The president's daughter…

264
00:17:17,407 --> 00:17:20,377
The president's daughter
is Mariko Nose, right?

265
00:17:21,778 --> 00:17:22,980
Oh, well,

266
00:17:23,046 --> 00:17:25,816
there is witness information
that the victim, Tateiwa,

267
00:17:25,883 --> 00:17:27,584
and Mariko Nose
were arguing in the company

268
00:17:27,651 --> 00:17:29,586
on the day of the incident.

269
00:17:30,454 --> 00:17:33,123
Does it mean those two
being in the same department

270
00:17:33,190 --> 00:17:34,925
has some connection?

271
00:17:37,461 --> 00:17:41,298
SOTA YUKIMURA

272
00:17:52,609 --> 00:17:54,711
You seem like you like sweets.

273
00:17:54,778 --> 00:17:56,547
-I do.
-I'm glad.

274
00:17:57,648 --> 00:17:59,950
I'm going to freshen up.

275
00:18:00,017 --> 00:18:02,619
-Straight out, second door on the right.
-Okay.

276
00:18:07,224 --> 00:18:10,194
You brought someone home to us, huh?

277
00:18:10,260 --> 00:18:11,161
First time ever.

278
00:18:12,763 --> 00:18:14,565
He just came without asking.

279
00:18:19,970 --> 00:18:22,806
I'm sorry for forcing you
to come with me today.

280
00:18:22,873 --> 00:18:23,941
Not at all.

281
00:18:25,075 --> 00:18:25,943
I enjoyed it.

282
00:18:27,044 --> 00:18:28,445
Liar.

283
00:18:29,346 --> 00:18:30,614
You actually hated it, didn't you?

284
00:18:31,648 --> 00:18:35,886
I didn't hate it,
I was just quite nervous.

285
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:38,255
Of course you were.

286
00:18:38,922 --> 00:18:40,157
I'm sorry.

287
00:18:41,992 --> 00:18:44,895
But you fit in surprisingly well.

288
00:18:45,762 --> 00:18:46,930
Me?

289
00:18:46,997 --> 00:18:48,765
I was really out of place.

290
00:18:48,832 --> 00:18:51,401
No, you fit in better than me.

291
00:18:56,373 --> 00:18:58,342
You are a mysterious person.

292
00:19:00,877 --> 00:19:02,913
Like someone who always
has perfect timing.

293
00:19:05,215 --> 00:19:08,685
You give me what I want, when I want it.

294
00:19:08,752 --> 00:19:11,221
You appear at the perfect time.

295
00:19:12,823 --> 00:19:13,890
Really?

296
00:19:15,359 --> 00:19:16,193
Yes.

297
00:19:20,964 --> 00:19:22,232
Like a wall.

298
00:19:25,168 --> 00:19:27,504
I just notice that you're there.

299
00:19:27,571 --> 00:19:29,072
But whether you're actually there or not

300
00:19:30,007 --> 00:19:31,074
doesn't really bother me.

301
00:19:36,079 --> 00:19:37,114
Sorry.

302
00:19:38,048 --> 00:19:39,983
Does "sorry" mean that was an insult?

303
00:19:40,050 --> 00:19:42,619
No, that's not what I meant at all.

304
00:19:47,557 --> 00:19:49,860
Is the birthday party an annual tradition?

305
00:19:51,395 --> 00:19:52,262
Yes.

306
00:19:53,664 --> 00:19:56,800
It's easy to just refuse to go, but…

307
00:19:59,069 --> 00:19:59,903
Actually…

308
00:20:01,772 --> 00:20:05,142
when I was a kid, I hated it so much
that I ran away from home.

309
00:20:08,512 --> 00:20:09,579
But today,

310
00:20:10,380 --> 00:20:12,349
I've decided to definitely go home

311
00:20:12,416 --> 00:20:14,151
because I want to celebrate my mother.

312
00:20:14,951 --> 00:20:16,520
Your mother?

313
00:20:16,586 --> 00:20:17,487
Yes.

314
00:20:19,856 --> 00:20:24,995
A birthday is the day a mother worked hard
to give birth to her child, right?

315
00:20:26,563 --> 00:20:29,032
If anything, that is more precious.

316
00:20:30,667 --> 00:20:31,535
So, the gathering earlier

317
00:20:32,903 --> 00:20:34,338
was a celebration for my mother.

318
00:20:39,242 --> 00:20:41,111
You really are kind, Mariko.

319
00:20:42,045 --> 00:20:43,046
What, how?

320
00:20:43,847 --> 00:20:45,782
I just want to put pressure on my father.

321
00:20:46,516 --> 00:20:48,185
To show him that I won't forgive him
if he forgets my mother.

322
00:20:50,420 --> 00:20:51,488
It's a spiteful way of thinking.

323
00:20:53,223 --> 00:20:57,327
My dad probably thinks
it's annoying deep down too.

324
00:20:57,394 --> 00:20:58,895
I don't think so.

325
00:21:00,297 --> 00:21:04,434
Because every year, only on this day,
he comes home without scheduling any work.

326
00:21:05,035 --> 00:21:08,405
He just really wants
to celebrate your birthday.

327
00:21:10,707 --> 00:21:12,676
How do you know something like that?

328
00:21:14,911 --> 00:21:15,846
Ah…

329
00:21:17,247 --> 00:21:20,417
I just thought that must be the case.

330
00:21:22,386 --> 00:21:23,253
Wait a second.

331
00:21:30,927 --> 00:21:32,262
Thank you for waiting.

332
00:21:32,929 --> 00:21:35,632
-Thank you.
-Thank you very much.

333
00:21:41,772 --> 00:21:42,606
Um…

334
00:21:44,207 --> 00:21:45,108
Here.

335
00:21:47,310 --> 00:21:49,079
I didn't get you a birthday present, so…

336
00:21:51,915 --> 00:21:55,218
-I'm sorry. I've overstepped.
-Oh, no.

337
00:21:56,853 --> 00:21:57,721
Thank you.

338
00:22:06,129 --> 00:22:08,432
Is the gathering
the only thing you do to celebrate?

339
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:11,701
Yes.

340
00:22:12,536 --> 00:22:15,505
In the first place, I hate birthdays.

341
00:22:17,474 --> 00:22:18,942
I'm not good with events and things.

342
00:22:21,378 --> 00:22:22,245
I see.

343
00:22:33,223 --> 00:22:34,958
<i>Today is Mariko's birthday.</i>

344
00:22:36,560 --> 00:22:39,329
<i>I also attended the annual birthday party</i>
<i>held at her family's house</i>

345
00:22:40,130 --> 00:22:42,532
<i>this year.</i>

346
00:23:00,851 --> 00:23:02,185
<i>Ever since that year,</i>

347
00:23:03,086 --> 00:23:05,822
<i>Mariko's always hated her birthday.</i>

348
00:23:48,532 --> 00:23:51,568
So this is your spot, huh?

349
00:23:51,635 --> 00:23:53,403
Yes. I've been coming a lot lately.

350
00:23:53,470 --> 00:23:54,404
Wow…

351
00:23:54,471 --> 00:23:55,805
Oh, Mariko, welcome.

352
00:23:55,872 --> 00:23:57,807
-Oh…
-Oh…

353
00:23:59,809 --> 00:24:01,111
Hello.

354
00:24:03,813 --> 00:24:07,717
Mariko and Yukimura
went to the same university?

355
00:24:07,784 --> 00:24:09,986
Yes, it feels like
a fateful reunion, doesn't it?

356
00:24:10,053 --> 00:24:11,955
Well, I don't know about fate.

357
00:24:12,022 --> 00:24:14,157
How long have you been running this shop?

358
00:24:14,224 --> 00:24:16,493
Since last autumn. Almost a year now.

359
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:19,129
Wow, I didn't notice it at all.

360
00:24:19,195 --> 00:24:21,798
You've been coming
since I first opened, right, Mariko?

361
00:24:21,865 --> 00:24:23,366
I suppose so.

362
00:24:24,267 --> 00:24:25,168
What?

363
00:24:25,669 --> 00:24:30,874
Does that mean you knew Mariko
all this time, but never spoke to her?

364
00:24:31,575 --> 00:24:33,343
-Isn't that creepy?
-What? That'd be scary.

365
00:24:33,910 --> 00:24:35,045
I was being considerate.

366
00:24:35,111 --> 00:24:38,148
But Mariko didn't know, right?

367
00:24:38,214 --> 00:24:40,951
To be known one-sidedly…

368
00:24:41,017 --> 00:24:43,386
Wouldn't you want him
to just speak to you already?

369
00:24:44,821 --> 00:24:48,925
No, um, I was glad he left me alone.

370
00:24:49,492 --> 00:24:52,362
If he had spoken to me,
I probably would have stopped coming.

371
00:24:52,429 --> 00:24:54,664
No way, what? But it's okay now, right?

372
00:24:56,399 --> 00:24:57,567
Yes, it's fine.

373
00:24:57,634 --> 00:25:00,370
Oh, thank goodness. That startled me.

374
00:25:00,437 --> 00:25:02,439
I thought Sota would get mad at me.

375
00:25:03,006 --> 00:25:04,140
What? Yukimura?

376
00:25:05,208 --> 00:25:07,444
Oh, no, uh, just talking to myself, yes.

377
00:25:09,312 --> 00:25:12,148
-Excuse me.
-Oh, yes? I'm coming.

378
00:25:12,215 --> 00:25:13,984
Well then, enjoy.

379
00:25:15,251 --> 00:25:16,786
-One person, right?
-Yes.

380
00:25:18,355 --> 00:25:21,424
He's Yukimura's friend, right?

381
00:25:21,992 --> 00:25:24,961
-They're pretty different…
-Yes.

382
00:25:25,695 --> 00:25:27,530
But he doesn't seem like a bad person.

383
00:25:29,165 --> 00:25:30,367
That's true.

384
00:25:42,445 --> 00:25:43,713
Manager.

385
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:45,949
Please take a look at this.

386
00:25:46,016 --> 00:25:48,685
Just leave those. I'll check later.

387
00:25:50,954 --> 00:25:52,989
Izumi, um…

388
00:25:53,056 --> 00:25:54,224
What is it?

389
00:25:54,290 --> 00:25:55,125
Did you have a fight?

390
00:25:55,692 --> 00:25:56,893
No.

391
00:25:57,861 --> 00:26:00,330
It's not that. It's just that
the Keihin Department Store samples…

392
00:26:00,397 --> 00:26:02,599
This is bad! Mariko!

393
00:26:02,666 --> 00:26:03,967
-Its really bad!
-What's wrong?

394
00:26:04,868 --> 00:26:08,705
Yukimura is suspected in Tateiwa's case.

395
00:26:09,305 --> 00:26:10,173
What?

396
00:26:10,240 --> 00:26:13,543
It seems the police
are questioning our employees.

397
00:26:13,610 --> 00:26:17,113
Several people said
they were asked about Yukimura.

398
00:26:26,022 --> 00:26:28,191
-Sorry for doing this during work hours.
-No.

399
00:26:28,892 --> 00:26:30,560
I haven't taken my lunch break yet.

400
00:26:30,627 --> 00:26:31,728
It's perfect timing.

401
00:26:33,063 --> 00:26:34,164
Have a seat.

402
00:26:35,665 --> 00:26:38,134
Since I didn't hear from you,

403
00:26:38,902 --> 00:26:40,970
I thought I might have been rejected.

404
00:26:41,504 --> 00:26:44,974
I'm sorry. I've been very busy
with some things.

405
00:26:46,042 --> 00:26:48,278
Your work seems busy.

406
00:26:50,313 --> 00:26:52,682
There is one thing I wanted to ask.

407
00:26:52,749 --> 00:26:55,685
Yes, ask me anything.

408
00:26:57,754 --> 00:27:01,858
Is it true that an employee of ours
is suspected in Tateiwa's case?

409
00:27:05,995 --> 00:27:08,932
Man, so that's what this is about…

410
00:27:11,835 --> 00:27:15,705
The reason you agreed to have a meal
with me is because I'm a police officer.

411
00:27:15,772 --> 00:27:16,940
Oh, no, um…

412
00:27:20,777 --> 00:27:21,611
I'm sorry.

413
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:25,014
You are a straightforward person.

414
00:27:26,549 --> 00:27:29,152
Since it's under investigation,
I can't speak in detail,

415
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:33,423
but we are looking
at various possibilities.

416
00:27:33,490 --> 00:27:36,960
I am truly sorry about Tateiwa.

417
00:27:37,794 --> 00:27:40,997
But it is impossible
that the culprit is my subordinate.

418
00:27:41,931 --> 00:27:43,600
I didn't mention this,

419
00:27:43,666 --> 00:27:46,469
but I was having a dispute
with Mr. Tateiwa over work,

420
00:27:46,536 --> 00:27:48,838
and we had an argument
right before the incident.

421
00:27:48,905 --> 00:27:52,242
Isn't it because of that
my subordinates are suspected?

422
00:27:52,308 --> 00:27:56,713
Or do the police think my subordinates
also had a bad relationship with Tateiwa?

423
00:27:57,680 --> 00:27:59,883
It was my personal issue.

424
00:27:59,949 --> 00:28:01,451
My subordinates have nothing
to do with it.

425
00:28:03,686 --> 00:28:06,422
We just need to investigate
from all angles,

426
00:28:07,023 --> 00:28:08,691
so please don't worry.

427
00:28:11,161 --> 00:28:13,897
Of course. I'm sorry.

428
00:28:16,266 --> 00:28:18,902
You are a good boss, Mariko.

429
00:28:20,837 --> 00:28:25,008
To be so desperate
to protect your subordinates…

430
00:28:34,350 --> 00:28:35,985
It's just as your grandmother said.

431
00:28:37,554 --> 00:28:39,389
Mariko isn't very sociable,

432
00:28:40,490 --> 00:28:42,091
but she is a kind child.

433
00:28:43,193 --> 00:28:45,295
She told me that many times before we met.

434
00:28:46,930 --> 00:28:48,231
She said that?

435
00:28:50,233 --> 00:28:51,868
You're a lot like your mother.

436
00:28:54,404 --> 00:28:56,506
She was a quiet person,

437
00:28:58,474 --> 00:28:59,876
but she was like a sunflower

438
00:29:01,077 --> 00:29:02,679
blooming in the family.

439
00:29:06,449 --> 00:29:08,852
You're exactly like her.

440
00:29:13,389 --> 00:29:15,024
My grandmother told you all that?

441
00:29:17,827 --> 00:29:19,796
You might have heard,

442
00:29:19,863 --> 00:29:24,701
but my mother died
in an accident when I was seven.

443
00:29:30,573 --> 00:29:33,309
That must have been very sad for you.

444
00:29:35,044 --> 00:29:37,680
I'm not sure what to say…

445
00:29:37,747 --> 00:29:39,449
Oh, no, it's okay.

446
00:29:40,383 --> 00:29:42,485
I'm a full adult now.

447
00:29:43,753 --> 00:29:45,288
It's something from the past.

448
00:29:53,529 --> 00:29:56,099
How about finding a new place?

449
00:29:57,433 --> 00:30:01,204
Instead of the past, creating a new future

450
00:30:02,472 --> 00:30:04,741
might be better for you.

451
00:30:07,677 --> 00:30:09,512
That was weird, wasn't it…

452
00:30:09,579 --> 00:30:11,247
-Oh, no…
-I'm sorry.

453
00:30:12,949 --> 00:30:15,318
<i>I want Mariko to smile.</i>

454
00:30:19,455 --> 00:30:22,992
<i>If that smile were to belong</i>
<i>to someone else…</i>

455
00:30:33,670 --> 00:30:35,471
NEW ASSETS

456
00:30:39,309 --> 00:30:40,310
HELLO, THIS IS KIKUTA.

457
00:30:40,376 --> 00:30:42,045
I SHOULD BE ABLE TO REPORT BACK
ON THE MATTER YOU REQUESTED TOMORROW.

458
00:30:42,111 --> 00:30:44,447
KIKUTA PRIVATE INVESTIGATION

459
00:30:45,214 --> 00:30:46,349
What?

460
00:30:47,750 --> 00:30:49,118
A detective agency?

461
00:31:05,368 --> 00:31:06,970
Good work, everyone. I'm heading out.

462
00:31:07,036 --> 00:31:08,571
-Goodbye.
-Thank you.

463
00:31:11,407 --> 00:31:13,076
FATHER

464
00:31:14,277 --> 00:31:15,178
Yes?

465
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:18,648
Understood.

466
00:31:19,182 --> 00:31:20,016
Okay.

467
00:31:23,353 --> 00:31:24,220
Excuse me.

468
00:31:24,921 --> 00:31:28,024
Sorry, this isn't about work.

469
00:31:33,896 --> 00:31:34,731
What is it?

470
00:31:35,465 --> 00:31:37,100
Sayuri asked me to give this to you.

471
00:31:45,041 --> 00:31:46,676
This is Mom's…

472
00:31:47,276 --> 00:31:49,379
She had it repaired to fit your size.

473
00:31:50,246 --> 00:31:51,914
Contact her and thank her.

474
00:32:02,191 --> 00:32:03,659
I didn't ask for this…

475
00:32:06,863 --> 00:32:08,031
I didn't ask for this!

476
00:32:08,598 --> 00:32:10,366
Why would she do this without asking?

477
00:32:11,134 --> 00:32:13,836
Why is she touching Mom's things
without permission?

478
00:32:15,638 --> 00:32:17,640
Sayuri was only thinking of you--

479
00:32:17,707 --> 00:32:20,176
I'm saying that it's selfish!

480
00:32:24,947 --> 00:32:26,115
Mariko.

481
00:32:35,825 --> 00:32:37,093
Ah, manager.

482
00:32:40,730 --> 00:32:41,798
What's wrong?

483
00:32:42,632 --> 00:32:43,533
It's nothing.

484
00:33:07,857 --> 00:33:09,859
<i>Happy birthday.</i>

485
00:33:09,926 --> 00:33:10,793
<i>Happy birthday, Mariko.</i>

486
00:33:13,629 --> 00:33:14,530
This is wrong.

487
00:33:17,133 --> 00:33:18,835
This is all wrong!

488
00:33:19,969 --> 00:33:20,937
Mariko!

489
00:33:31,414 --> 00:33:32,415
Mom…

490
00:33:37,386 --> 00:33:38,821
<i>Mom…</i>

491
00:34:05,348 --> 00:34:06,382
Mariko…

492
00:34:07,917 --> 00:34:08,818
Why?

493
00:34:11,020 --> 00:34:12,288
I felt like you were crying.

494
00:34:16,926 --> 00:34:18,027
What?

495
00:34:19,428 --> 00:34:21,364
What are you saying?

496
00:34:21,964 --> 00:34:23,533
Wait, how did you know I was here?

497
00:34:26,502 --> 00:34:28,237
I just happened to be passing by.

498
00:34:28,304 --> 00:34:29,472
You just happened to?

499
00:34:30,173 --> 00:34:31,774
You're completely out of breath.

500
00:34:37,847 --> 00:34:39,415
What are you?

501
00:34:41,551 --> 00:34:43,753
Giving me the flower my mother liked

502
00:34:44,420 --> 00:34:45,855
and even knowing this park…

503
00:34:52,261 --> 00:34:53,629
This is a special place, isn't it?

504
00:34:59,268 --> 00:35:00,136
This is…

505
00:35:02,572 --> 00:35:04,207
my sanctuary.

506
00:35:09,278 --> 00:35:10,446
When I was a kid,

507
00:35:12,982 --> 00:35:15,218
whenever something bad happened,
I always came here.

508
00:35:18,054 --> 00:35:20,256
Because when I was here,
Mom would come to get me.

509
00:35:24,827 --> 00:35:26,429
The year my mother died…

510
00:35:29,398 --> 00:35:31,467
I ran away from home
because I hated the birthday party.

511
00:35:34,370 --> 00:35:35,371
Alone.

512
00:35:39,942 --> 00:35:43,312
Even though I knew Mom
wouldn't come to get me anymore.

513
00:35:48,484 --> 00:35:49,719
But strangely…

514
00:35:51,687 --> 00:35:56,359
being here made me feel
like Mom was nearby,

515
00:35:59,028 --> 00:36:00,062
and I was able

516
00:36:01,831 --> 00:36:03,666
to return home safely

517
00:36:04,567 --> 00:36:05,835
without losing my way.

518
00:36:13,376 --> 00:36:14,610
Wait…

519
00:36:14,677 --> 00:36:16,812
Why am I telling you this?

520
00:36:23,286 --> 00:36:24,654
You hate birthdays

521
00:36:26,489 --> 00:36:28,057
because they remind you of your mother.

522
00:36:30,126 --> 00:36:31,727
You hate the birthday parties

523
00:36:33,262 --> 00:36:36,065
because you loved the birthday parties

524
00:36:37,700 --> 00:36:39,035
your mother threw for you.

525
00:36:44,040 --> 00:36:45,141
My father is right.

526
00:36:46,942 --> 00:36:48,411
I'm acting like a fool at my age.

527
00:36:48,477 --> 00:36:50,212
I shouldn't even worry about it.

528
00:36:50,279 --> 00:36:51,213
That doesn't matter.

529
00:36:54,283 --> 00:36:55,851
No matter how old you get…

530
00:36:59,121 --> 00:37:01,157
or how many years pass…

531
00:37:03,159 --> 00:37:04,994
you cannot forget sad things.

532
00:37:07,830 --> 00:37:09,131
You just get better…

533
00:37:11,434 --> 00:37:12,935
at pretending to forget,

534
00:37:14,170 --> 00:37:15,805
better at lying that you forgot.

535
00:37:19,408 --> 00:37:23,079
I don't think you need to force yourself

536
00:37:24,914 --> 00:37:26,515
to share your true feelings

537
00:37:28,017 --> 00:37:29,885
or show your weakness to anyone.

538
00:37:31,320 --> 00:37:33,255
I think there are people
who are not good at that.

539
00:37:36,525 --> 00:37:37,393
But…

540
00:37:41,130 --> 00:37:43,399
please just at least
don't lie to yourself.

541
00:37:47,136 --> 00:37:48,671
The sadness…

542
00:37:50,339 --> 00:37:51,607
of losing your mother…

543
00:37:53,242 --> 00:37:54,410
the loneliness…

544
00:37:57,613 --> 00:38:00,850
Even the happiness of the birthday parties
she threw for you when you were a child…

545
00:38:03,753 --> 00:38:07,323
Please don't seal them away
and pretend they didn't exist.

546
00:38:11,527 --> 00:38:14,897
Please, keep them with you, always.

547
00:38:24,907 --> 00:38:26,542
I've never cried
in front of someone before.

548
00:38:29,712 --> 00:38:31,480
I'm a wall. It's okay.

549
00:38:34,250 --> 00:38:35,518
I'm just here.

550
00:38:37,820 --> 00:38:38,921
So don't worry.

551
00:38:41,123 --> 00:38:42,024
What is that?

552
00:39:51,060 --> 00:39:52,027
<i>This is strange.</i>

553
00:39:52,928 --> 00:39:55,197
<i>I thought I only wanted</i>
<i>to see Mariko smile, but…</i>

554
00:40:00,536 --> 00:40:01,770
Mom…

555
00:40:06,208 --> 00:40:07,877
That white one…

556
00:40:07,943 --> 00:40:11,146
The white cylindrical one
that's big at the top.

557
00:40:11,213 --> 00:40:13,549
Is it this one?

558
00:40:13,616 --> 00:40:15,718
No, not that. It's about this big…

559
00:40:44,547 --> 00:40:45,514
Mom?

560
00:41:11,907 --> 00:41:13,642
"I HELD A GRUDGE AGAINST THE ENTIRE WORLD"

561
00:41:13,709 --> 00:41:15,244
It's a good article.

562
00:41:15,978 --> 00:41:17,813
I hear it's selling pretty well.

563
00:41:18,647 --> 00:41:21,183
But they paid me almost nothing.

564
00:41:22,952 --> 00:41:24,453
This is you?

565
00:41:24,520 --> 00:41:26,388
It's a tough world, huh?

566
00:41:28,557 --> 00:41:30,926
I wonder if I have any other stories
that would sell for more.

567
00:41:36,365 --> 00:41:38,901
<i>A single factor</i>
<i>that exerts power is strong.</i>

568
00:41:40,235 --> 00:41:42,037
<i>-But…</i>
-…you like sweets.

569
00:41:42,104 --> 00:41:43,939
-I do.
-I'm glad.

570
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:47,376
I'm going to freshen up.

571
00:41:47,443 --> 00:41:50,245
-Straight out, second door on the right.
-Okay.

572
00:41:55,618 --> 00:41:57,252
What time are you coming home?

573
00:41:58,921 --> 00:41:59,989
Keito?

574
00:42:02,257 --> 00:42:03,859
Hey, Keito!

575
00:42:03,926 --> 00:42:07,663
<i>When multiple factors intertwine,</i>
<i>it becomes complex and troublesome.</i>

576
00:42:24,813 --> 00:42:25,681
No.

577
00:42:26,348 --> 00:42:28,283
Because I don't want
my daughter to hear about it.

578
00:42:29,218 --> 00:42:30,619
Yes.

579
00:42:30,686 --> 00:42:35,124
Talking again after getting out of prison,
he really is a plague.

580
00:42:37,526 --> 00:42:38,761
Thank you.

581
00:42:39,595 --> 00:42:40,496
Goodbye.

582
00:43:16,632 --> 00:43:18,701
AWARD

583
00:43:53,202 --> 00:43:54,136
Whoa…

584
00:44:03,612 --> 00:44:05,914
<i>There is still something in this family.</i>

585
00:44:06,882 --> 00:44:09,084
<i>Something that steals Mariko's smile.</i>

586
00:44:09,151 --> 00:44:10,919
PRESIDENT'S OFFICE

587
00:44:11,653 --> 00:44:13,489
<i>Is the risk factor an individual?</i>

588
00:44:14,156 --> 00:44:16,925
-<i>Or is it…</i>
-Yukimura.

589
00:44:19,895 --> 00:44:21,263
I need to talk to you.

590
00:44:53,262 --> 00:44:55,397
<i>I want Mariko to be happy.</i>

591
00:44:56,498 --> 00:44:57,566
<i>That's all.</i>

592
00:44:59,134 --> 00:45:01,703
What are you to Mariko?

593
00:45:01,770 --> 00:45:03,572
‎NEXT TIME…

594
00:45:08,143 --> 00:45:09,077
Subtitle translation by: Maslin Friedman

